# 🐼 Pandas Notes — Complete Cheat Sheet for Data Analytics

A beginner-friendly, well-organized reference for **Pandas** — the most popular Python library for data manipulation and analysis. Every section includes code + explanation. Perfect for quick revision or as a GitHub reference repo. ⭐

---

## 📦 1. Installation & Import

```python
pip install pandas
```

```python
import pandas as pd   # 'pd' is the standard alias used everywhere
```

> 💡 **Why pandas?** It gives you two powerful structures — `Series` (1D) and `DataFrame` (2D, like an Excel sheet) — to store and analyze data easily.

---

## 🧱 2. Core Data Structures

### 🔹 Series (1D array with labels)
```python
s = pd.Series([10, 20, 30, 40])
print(s)
```
👉 Creates a single column of data with an automatic index (0,1,2,3).

### 🔹 DataFrame (2D table)
```python
data = {
    "Name": ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"],
    "Age": [25, 30, 35],
    "City": ["NY", "LA", "SF"]
}
df = pd.DataFrame(data)
print(df)
```
👉 Creates a table-like structure where each **key** becomes a column and each **list value** becomes the rows.

---

## 📂 3. Reading & Writing Data

| Task | Code | Explanation |
|------|------|-------------|
| 📥 Read CSV | `df = pd.read_csv("file.csv")` | Loads a CSV file into a DataFrame |
| 📥 Read Excel | `df = pd.read_excel("file.xlsx")` | Loads an Excel sheet |
| 📥 Read JSON | `df = pd.read_json("file.json")` | Loads JSON data |
| 📤 Save CSV | `df.to_csv("out.csv", index=False)` | Saves DataFrame without writing row numbers |
| 📤 Save Excel | `df.to_excel("out.xlsx", index=False)` | Saves to Excel format |

> ⚠️ `index=False` prevents pandas from adding an extra unwanted index column when saving.

