## 🔍 4. Exploring Your Data (First Look)

```python
df.head()       # 👀 First 5 rows
df.tail(3)      # 👀 Last 3 rows
df.shape        # 📏 (rows, columns)
df.columns      # 🏷️ List of column names
df.info()       # ℹ️ Data types + non-null counts
df.describe()   # 📊 Statistical summary (mean, std, min, max...)
df.dtypes       # 🔢 Data type of each column
```

> 💡 **Always run `df.head()` and `df.info()` first** — this gives you an instant overview of structure, missing values, and data types.

---

## 🎯 5. Selecting Data

```python
df["Name"]              # 🔹 Select a single column → returns a Series
df[["Name", "Age"]]      # 🔹 Select multiple columns → returns a DataFrame

df.loc[0]                # 🔹 Select row by LABEL (index name)
df.iloc[0]               # 🔹 Select row by POSITION (numeric index)

df.loc[0, "Name"]        # 🔹 Specific cell by label
df.iloc[0, 1]            # 🔹 Specific cell by position
```

> 🆚 **`loc` vs `iloc`**
> - `loc` → uses **labels/names** (e.g., "Name", "A")
> - `iloc` → uses **integer positions** (e.g., 0, 1, 2)

---

## 🧹 6. Filtering Rows (Conditions)

```python
df[df["Age"] > 25]                       # ✅ Rows where Age > 25
df[(df["Age"] > 25) & (df["City"]=="NY")] # ✅ Multiple conditions (AND)
df[(df["Age"] > 25) | (df["City"]=="LA")] # ✅ Multiple conditions (OR)
df[df["Name"].isin(["Alice","Bob"])]      # ✅ Filter using a list of values
```

> 🔑 Use `&` for AND, `|` for OR — **not** `and`/`or` — and wrap each condition in `()`.

---

## 🧼 7. Handling Missing Data

```python
df.isnull()              # ❓ True/False for missing values
df.isnull().sum()        # 🔢 Count of missing values per column

df.dropna()               # 🗑️ Remove rows with any missing values
df.fillna(0)              # 🩹 Replace missing values with 0
df.fillna(df["Age"].mean())  # 🩹 Fill with column average (smart fill)
```

> ⚠️ **Never blindly drop missing data** — check how much you're losing first with `df.isnull().sum()`.

---

## ✏️ 8. Modifying Data

```python
df["Salary"] = [50000, 60000, 70000]      # ➕ Add new column

df["Age"] = df["Age"] + 1                  # 🔄 Update existing column

df.rename(columns={"Name": "Full Name"})   # ✏️ Rename column(s)

df.drop("City", axis=1)                    # ❌ Delete a column (axis=1 = column)
df.drop(0, axis=0)                         # ❌ Delete a row (axis=0 = row)
```

> 💡 **axis=0** → operates on **rows** | **axis=1** → operates on **columns**

---

## 🔢 9. Sorting & Ranking

```python
df.sort_values("Age")                     # ⬆️ Sort ascending by Age
df.sort_values("Age", ascending=False)    # ⬇️ Sort descending
df.sort_values(["Age","Name"])            # 🔢 Sort by multiple columns
```

---

## 📊 10. Grouping & Aggregation (Powerful!)

```python
df.groupby("City")["Salary"].mean()       # 📈 Avg salary per city
df.groupby("City")["Salary"].sum()        # ➕ Total salary per city
df.groupby("City").agg({"Salary":"mean", "Age":"max"})  # 🎯 Multiple aggregations
```

> 🧠 **Think of `groupby` like Excel's PivotTable** — it splits data into groups, applies a function, and combines the results.

---

## 🔗 11. Merging & Combining DataFrames

```python
pd.concat([df1, df2])                     # ➕ Stack DataFrames (rows on top of rows)

pd.merge(df1, df2, on="ID", how="inner")  # 🔗 SQL-style join on a common column
```

| `how` value | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `inner` | Only matching rows from both ✅ |
| `left`  | All from left + matches from right ⬅️ |
| `right` | All from right + matches from left ➡️ |
| `outer` | Everything from both, fill gaps with NaN 🔄 |

